From: cjl <Unknown>
Date: Saturday, August 5, 2017 at 8:44:34 PM UTC-5
Subject: P?S/8 SHELL device identification and related matters.
To: P?S/8 <Unknown>


With the discussion of some of the OS/8 device identification shortcomings, 
and as is per usual, OS/8 points the way to NOT go, so here is some points 
about the future of the P?S/8 SHELL's CORRECT direction.

P?S/8 as it currently exists we are calling the "basic" system.  Every 
system program uses the P?S/8 system device handler for all functions.

This is not as limited as it sounds at first glance:

A comparison of the two systems' device handler calls will reveal some 
interesting things:

OS/8.

    JMS I    (7607)
    FUNCTION
    BUFFER
    RECORD
    error-return
    good-return

P?S/8

    JMS I    (7640)
    BUFFER
    FUNCTION
    BLOCK
    good-return

The call to the P?S/8 system device handler is at the address that is the 
same code as the SZA CLA instruction.  For many code-squeezing situations, 
it is trivial to make it true that a nearby instruction can be used as the 
pointer thus saving a page zero literal.  [The P?S/8 keyboard monitor 
literally does that; it really isn't hard in the scheme of code-bumming 
techniques].  OS/8 uses 7607 which has no particular utility; as an 
instruction it is a group III EAE instruction.

The BUFFER argument is identical in both systems - the 12-bit read or write 
buffer for the operation.

P?S/8 addresses to the nearest logical 128-word block.  OS/8 to the nearest 
256 word record with a kludge:  The send-half of records can be destroyed 
with impunity.  In P?S/8 you must preserve things.  In the case of the 
P?S/8 RK8E handlers, the handler uses an internal buffer to make that 
viable, but only if the case comes up.  In all cases of "double-even" 
blocks, the throughput is identical in both systems.  [Note: Some OS/8 RK8E 
handlers have a bug:  The "check" bit is set on every HEAD change instead 
of every TRACK change.]

P?S/8 conversion programs such as OS8CON depend on the 2:1 relationship.  
The OS/8 DECtape handlers just double up the past record number to become 
the block number..  As such, these programs have to use compatible 
devices such as in OS/8 DTA1: is logical unit 1 on a P?S/8 DECape-based 
system.

The more important part is the difference in the FUNCTION word.

All of the bits are identical except that in OS/8 bits [9-11] were INTENDED 
to mean what they do in P?S/8, but because of management interference, this 
was never to happen.  As in the PDP-10 TOPS-10 support, there is a DTA 
handler with logical units 0-7; OS/8 just has a DTA0: handler and a DTA1: 
handler, etc. up to a DTA7: handler.  And there are only 14 handlers 
maximum [DSK is a dummy to be assigned to one of the others and in some 
systems, the co-resient handlers are problematic.  A rationally configured 
OS/8 system for RK8E had better NOT use the SYS: handler and the RKA0: [and 
similarly the RKB0: handler] because if you boot to one of the other 
drives, they become RKAx and RKBx where x is the boot drive and NOT RKA0 
and RKA1.  Many find this out too late!

Later, OS/8 assed the feeble notion of bit[11] is a "recommendation" from 
the caller regarding the initial search direction bit for tape-based 
systems.  There are multiple problems with this nonsense:

a) It is a static guess, and as such there are only 3-4 of them throughout 
OS/8.  This missed at least 20 times as many DYNAMIC situations.

b) It should be possible to write a better handler that just knows what to 
do.  Someone once wrote a TC01/TC08 handler that self-guesses and just 
ignores the bit.

P?S/8 tape handlers all guess themselves, and that means ALL possibilities 
are exploited dynamically.

Had OS/8 actually implemented the logical unit bits, there would be a lot 
less need for device slots to be filled.  P?S/8 will ALWAYS support the 
logical device unit concept, thus inherently it is trivial to out-class 
OS/8 when even only two such devices are supported in any configuration [as 
two handlers are doing the job OS/8 needs SIXTEEN to accomplish which is 
beyond the OS/8 capability.  Moreover, you can prove that using 
non-structured handlers is largely a kludge.  Better to write utility 
programs for the few cases that work, such as to have support for .5 inch 
magtape.    You can define a far better result totally within the purview 
of a utility program, etc.  This is why IOMEC didn't bother with this level 
of support.  I worked for 1.5 years for a company that was heavily 
dependent on various IOMEC drives; utility programs were written quite 
successfully; the TM8E could not compete and came out later, etc.

The console handling of OS/8 is really program-rolls-its-own.  The handlers 
do exist, but most programs' output doesn't use them!  Instead, rigorously 
ALL conforming programs use the correct console support, which includes:

a) Full support for Control-S/Control-Q reverse protocol.

b) Full support for DECmates.  If a program is not running on a PDP-8, it 
is impossible to have program abort work as on the PDP-8.  P?S/8 supports a 
logical abort bit.  A program that is ACTUALLY running on a PDP-8 can use 
the usual method of noticing the latest character is Control-C AND have the 
keyboard flag be preserved [using KRS] but if running on a DECmate, instead 
you MUST use KRB, and then upon an abort exist, perform the instruction ISZ 
I (7611).  The P?S/8 keyboard monitor works both ways, etc.  Thus, there is 
no point in having those two handlers.

c) P?S/8 supports the unique concept of the Logical Console Overlay where 
all conforming programs call resident routines optionally loaded into the 
highest field of memory.  JMS to page zero locations is used for all 
functions.  This way, the hardware for the console can be totally different 
and no programs are ever changed.  This has allowed such as remote device 
40/41 terminals to be used, or terminal emulator devices such as the VT8E 
or the PDP-12 VR12/14 screen.  Complete error messages can come out 
elegantly on the console screen to allow retries or aborts, etc.  The 
console overlay configuration is an independent concept of generically 
calling the console at all.  the default console configuration is identical 
to the normal 03/04 standard handling, so only if an error occurs would you 
even know it was present [other than by checking the obvious memory usage]. 
A configuration was created to allow a researcher to abort the 
data-acquisition program from the analog equipment by hooking a push-button 
to a DR8E input bit in a LAB-8/E configuration.  The PDP-12 can do an 
analogous function.  Either way, the logical equivalent of a Control-C 
being pressed was passed back to P?S/8 when the program aborted, etc.

The Overlay also supports LPT redirection from the default standard of 
device.  [Note: OS/8 BATCH is NOT device-independent.  It MUST use the 
device 66 LPT:  On the newer releases for DECmates, this was changed to 
device 32/33]

Calling programs can just output to the logical LPT and the JMS calls do 
all of the work, including print-buffering and reverse-channel protocol if 
this is actually a serial printer.  An Interrupt-driven program MUST use 
the provided reverse-channel call [otherwise optional] to field the 
protocol, but this is because interrupt handling must ALWAYS first use the 
logical console overlay interrupt [done via a JMS 0 in imitation of the 
hardware] and then the
logical "flags" have to be checked, etc.

The only additional problem in P?S/8 is the need to support paper-tape 
input and output.  towards that end, P?S/8 BIN handles all of the 
variations for binary paper-tapes.  The PTUTIL program currently under 
development will handle all of the source file analogous situations.  P/S/8 
historically has been written by individuals who HATE paper-tape and the 
original configuration was a PDP-8 with 4K, EAE, TC01 and one TU55 and the 
console was a KSR-35.  [We could only read paper-tapes by "borrowing" the 
console from the PDP-8/I mentioned elsewhere as a "midnight" operation.

The only reason for the renewed interest in paper-tape is so the SIMH 
simulator can work without resorting to using OS/8 and THEN OS8CON to move 
source files around.  When PTUTIL is done, the process can literally be 
automated to create P?S/8 PAL listing files without any manual input.  [The 
SIMH simulator supports a feature to cause auto-exit by executing such as 
6050, and the rest can be deal with using P?S/8 BATCH to create an infinite 
loop of the BATCH file calling itself.  P?S/8 BATCH has two modes, one just 
is lost if something reboots, and the other breakpoints the batch so it 
continues after the next reboot, which could be on another machine!

Once this is done, it would be quite contrived to claim there is need for a 
"handler" in any sense.  paper-tapes should be relegated to utility 
programs; wasting handler slots is not a good design.

Thus, the only conceptual limitation of the basic system is when you have 
configurations such as DECtapes and disks; the P?S/8 SHELL will use 
non-system handlers to get around this, etc.

Since there will be by definition far less handlers needed, each handler 
can have more information associated with it.  No need for kludges such as 
the device lengthi is stored inside of PIP.SV on the system device, and 
RESORC.SV has to have kludges to figure out the rest.

The handlers will have entries with such as device size if knowable, OR you 
interrogate the non-system handler to find out what applies CURRENTLY.  
This would apply to an RX01/02/03 non-system handler analogous to OS/8 
currently, and also to DECtapes where at least two sizes of DECtapes are in 
common use [2702 blocks and 3300 blocks.  Since the handlers have so much 
more room to implement the functions, a return current length function can 
be defined:  Just perform a search; when it successes record the latest 
block, increment until an END error occurs.  On the PDP-12, in extended 
operations mode, you can wait until a read completes without error and 
judiciously use good starsting points to make the process take minimum time 
[other than winding the tape up].  Patch the system to a fixed size if you 
never use these tapes, but OS/9 does not support the concept of more than 
one at a time.  Anything RESORC ought to know belongs in the same area, 
etc. Logical unit info can be limited to the EXCEPTIONS to the assumptions 
of 0 through 7 instead of listing out all of the similar yet different 
DTAx: entries of OS/8, etc.

The P?S/8 handler does not have an error return.  This is a fallacy of OS/8 
because of the basic concept being flawed from day one:

The call to load a system program final memory segment is in field 1 at 
7666.  There is every possibility this attempt can fail.  [Note: On actual 
DECtape systems, some bad experiences have occurred of late.  The OS/8 
corresponding handlers IGNORE errors while searching!  This is an accident 
waiting to happen.]  The P?S/8 handler stops on ALL errors.  That way, you 
can know your tape system is failing and have time to recover the data, not 
find out too late that it is no non-recoverable.

Thus, compare to the P?S/8 equivalent, which is an embedded call to the 
handler at 07632.  If there is an error, it is NOT going to return.  The 
only recourse is to RETY.  But it CAN succeed, in which case it is the 
proper thing to do to continue.

But in OS/8, you get a FATAL HLT that if you press CONTINUE, you are 
playing Russian Roulette with your system.  You are going to execute code 
known to have not loaded correctly!

In P?S/8 without the Logical Console Overlay, you have to press CONTINUE.  
If the Overlay is enabled, an elegant retry or abort operation is used to 
confront the user/operator.

Thus, by definition, the OS/8 system device handler concept is frawed and 
unfixable.  There simply isn't enough space in that call to recover, etc.

The P?S/8 SHELL will use the system device handler just for inter-component 
usage, such as to load non-system handlers, etc.

The non-system handler DOES have an error return:


    JMS I    (HANDLER)
    PTR
    error-return
    good-return

PTR points to a separate arg grouping [and it can be on page zero which can 
be useful]

PTR,    BUFFER
    FUNCTION
    BLOCK-LOW
    BLOCK-HIGH

Because the block argument is double-precision, handler support extends in 
theory to devices 2048 times as large as OS/8.  As a practical matter, an 
entire RK05 pack is one device, etc.  A contrived confituration could treat 
the hardware as an RK05F.  That means that there are blocks 000000-625777 
on one logical device.

The reason for this design is because it is reasonable that ALL programs 
calling a non-system handler have the inherent situation to properly handle 
errors, at least minimally.  No handlers are involved in calling handlers, 
etc.

A nice side effect of P?S/8 design is all input or output buffers are 
multiples of pages, not double-pages.  Programs such as BATCH only need a 
single page input buffer because the actual data is hardly used; a BATCH 
file is seldom even a complete one-page block, etc.

OS/8 cannot take advantage of any ODD number of pages in a buffer.  Due to 
the general stupidity regarding x7600-x7777, the value of an entire field 
32 pages is not possible.  [Note: Unlike OS/8, P?S/8 non-system handlers 
are FIELD and page relocatable.]

The P?S/8 memory model is all programs get 00000-07577, same as OS/8, but 
then 10000-onward up to the last field of memory inclusive. In many systems 
that is literally the case and could be as much as tne entirety of field 7.

Depending on optional configuration considerations, at worst all of the 
highest field is reserved, but might only be portions.

For example, if a system device handler has the requirement of an 
extended-memory section it always occupies x6000-x7777, where x is the 
highest actual field.  If you reboot on a different machine it will be 
dynamically determined there, etc.

If the Logical Console Overlay is not enabled, the end of memory would be 
x5777.  For programs such as P?S/8 PAL, that extra 3K for more symbols can 
be quite important.  15-bit addressing programs such as Fortran IV can take 
advantage of this as well.  There are no sore points such as OS/8 requiring 
12K for a device.  The 15-bit linear addressing space starts at 10000, not 
20000 [and no kludges to make even that work!].


Non-system handler calls will be developed for custom functions.  the 
current known one is return device size, say ask for block 7777,7777.  
Always takes the error return and the address within the handler where the 
length is in double-precision is in the AC.  Virtually any number of 
functions can be defined in the future as things such as this are easily 
open-ended, etc.

Good for all DECtapes, LINCtapes, and RX devices, etc.  If not ready, 
returns 0000;0000's address because it is still unknown, etc.

Loading the last segment is to write to the P?S/8 standard scratch area for 
all of field 0 [blocks 20-37 and 40-57] which is defined even in the basic 
P?S/8 system for analogous purposes.  However, if a program does not 
indicate the preservation of memory [the norm] then if there is memory over 
and above the highest field of the program load, instead load the field 0 
image into y0000-y7577 where y is the highest available field [which might 
be X-1 where x is the highest field assuming the worst-case scenario of 
needing an extended-length handler] and then just moving the contents of 
field 0 to field 0 from field Y, etc.  Only if the memory is either truly 
small, or the program specifies to not do this, would it be done by the 
brute-force method.  In general if you actually have more than 4K, expect 
all programs to load more rapidly. [P?S/8 basic is 4K-oriented.  P?S/8 
SHELL is give me as much memory as you have and do the best job possible 
oriented].

cjl







